    detail: 'The EPR paper presented a thought experiment: two particles interact and then fly apart. Quantum mechanics predicts that measuring a property of one particle (say, its spin) instantly determines the corresponding property of the other, no matter how far apart they are. EPR argued this was absurd — it would require instantaneous influence across space, violating relativity. They concluded that quantum mechanics must be incomplete: the particles must have had definite properties all along, described by "hidden variables" the theory ignored. Niels Bohr disputed this, but his rebuttal was widely seen as obscure. The debate remained philosophical until 1964 when John Bell turned it into a testable mathematical prediction — and 1982 when Alain Aspect\'s experiments proved Einstein wrong.',
    significance: 'Launched the study of quantum entanglement and quantum non-locality — concepts now central to quantum computing, quantum cryptography, and quantum teleportation.',
    equation: '|ψ⟩ = (1/√2)(|↑↓⟩ − |↓↑⟩)',
    equationLabel: 'A Bell state: maximally entangled state of two spin-½ particles',
    wowFact: 'Einstein called entanglement "spooky action at a distance" (spukhafte Fernwirkung) — a phrase he intended dismissively as evidence of quantum mechanics\' absurdity. The term became one of the most celebrated descriptions in physics and is now used approvingly by experimentalists who have confirmed the effect to extraordinary precision.',
