    detail: 'Quantum electrodynamics is the quantum field theory of electromagnetic interactions — how charged particles (like electrons) interact via photons. Earlier attempts had been plagued by infinities: when higher-order quantum corrections were calculated, the answers diverged. Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga each independently developed different mathematical techniques to handle these infinities by "renormalisation" — systematically absorbing them into the definitions of measured quantities like the electron\'s charge and mass. The resulting theory was extraordinarily precise. QED\'s prediction of the electron\'s magnetic moment (g-factor) agrees with direct measurement to 11 decimal places — the most precise agreement between theory and experiment in the history of science. Freeman Dyson proved all three approaches were mathematically equivalent.',
