    detail: 'Feynman\'s "sum over histories" approach proposed that the quantum amplitude for a particle to travel from A to B is computed by summing contributions from every conceivable path connecting them — including paths that loop back in time, travel faster than light, or go via the Andromeda galaxy. Each path contributes a phase factor e^(iS/ℏ) where S is the classical action along that path. For most everyday objects, all paths except the classical (least-action) path cancel by destructive interference — recovering classical mechanics. For quantum particles, however, many paths contribute significantly, producing genuinely quantum behaviour. This formulation is conceptually radical but computationally powerful, and forms the basis of modern quantum field theory and the Standard Model.',
    significance: 'Provides a physically intuitive picture of quantum mechanics (all paths are tried) and the most powerful computational framework for quantum field theory.',
    equation: '⟨x_f|e^{-iĤt/ℏ}|x_i⟩ = ∫ 𝒟[x] e^{iS[x]/ℏ}',
