    detail: 'BB84 (named for its authors and publication year) exploits a fundamental quantum mechanical property: measuring a quantum system inevitably disturbs it — and this disturbance is detectable. In the protocol, photons are sent polarised in one of four directions, encoded in two conjugate bases. The receiver measures each photon in a randomly chosen basis. After the transmission, they publicly compare which bases they used (not the results). Where they chose the same basis, the results should match perfectly. If an eavesdropper (Eve) has intercepted and re-sent photons, she will introduce detectable errors — because measuring a photon in the wrong basis destroys its original state. By checking a sample of their key for errors, Alice and Bob can detect any eavesdropping with arbitrarily high probability. The security is guaranteed by physics, not computational hardness.',
